        "id": 1060151
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-12-16T01:30:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "1) How does downgrading to a read lock to write work?\n\n2) Premature optimization is always a mistake. I\u0027d much rather spend our time making this correct than making VCN config writing in a hot loop work well (since it doesn\u0027t need to).\n\nI strongly recommend the simplest possible (technically sound) locking strategy for your first pass. I see a ton of code and complexity spent on these locks, and cleaner simpler code is more valuable than hyper-optimized locking. Even with all this locking already written, I would favor reworking it with a simpler approach. From the ReadWriteLock documentation:\n\n\"...In theory, the increase in concurrency permitted by the use of a read-write lock will lead to performance improvements over the use of a mutual exclusion lock. In practice this increase in concurrency will only be fully realized on a multi-processor, and then only if the access patterns for the shared data are suitable.\"",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 291,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 293,
        "endChar": 44
      },
